WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.626 --> 00:00:07.295
So whenever I tell people
I’m an Arctic scientist,

00:00:07.295 --> 00:00:11.633
the first thing that they always ask me
is: How cold is it up there?

00:00:11.675 --> 00:00:12.676
(Laughter)

00:00:12.676 --> 00:00:14.844
And yeah, the Arctic can get pretty cold.

00:00:14.928 --> 00:00:16.221
Trust me when I tell you

00:00:16.221 --> 00:00:20.600
that working outside at -40 degrees
is really, really challenging.

00:00:22.435 --> 00:00:23.937
But in the summer of 2019,

00:00:23.937 --> 00:00:25.855
it was anything but cold.

00:00:26.815 --> 00:00:29.985
So that summer I was working
with my research team

00:00:29.985 --> 00:00:32.862
in Alaska’s Yukon Kuskokwim Delta

00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:35.740
on the traditional lands
of the Yup’ik and Cup’ik people.

00:00:36.908 --> 00:00:40.870
And we were up there
hauling hundreds of pounds of equipment

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:44.749
across the tundra in the middle
of a record-breaking heatwave.

00:00:45.709 --> 00:00:47.919
It was 90 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:49.754
There was no breeze,

00:00:49.754 --> 00:00:51.006
nowhere to go for a shade

00:00:51.006 --> 00:00:55.468
and seemingly endless miles of tundra
as far as my eyes could see.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:57.804
To make matters worse,

00:00:57.804 --> 00:00:59.806
the land had drastically changed

00:00:59.806 --> 00:01:01.933
since we had been here
just one year before.

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:05.353
The ground was sinking
and it was cracking.

00:01:05.645 --> 00:01:09.190
In places, it was literally
collapsing beneath my feet.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.236
I’ve been working in the Arctic
for more than a decade,

00:01:13.236 --> 00:01:18.325
and I had never seen changes happening
this rapidly ever before.

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:23.079
The changes we saw were remarkable,

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:26.082
and they were also really concerning.

00:01:27.626 --> 00:01:30.420
But we were there  to measure changes
that we couldn’t see.

00:01:31.713 --> 00:01:35.008
We were there to fill a major gap
in our understanding

00:01:35.008 --> 00:01:38.970
of how the changing Arctic
is impacting the Earth’s climate.

00:01:40.263 --> 00:01:43.266
So we were installing what’s called
an “eddy covariance tower,”

00:01:43.266 --> 00:01:45.060
which is a series of instruments

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.812
that measures the exchange
of greenhouse gases,

00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:50.231
like carbon dioxide and methane,

00:01:50.231 --> 00:01:52.108
between the land and the atmosphere.

00:01:52.859 --> 00:01:55.403
It’s essentially like measuring
the Earth’s breath.

00:01:57.072 --> 00:02:00.158
And so the reason
the land around us was collapsing

00:02:00.158 --> 00:02:03.870
is because the once permanently
frozen ground called permafrost

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.080
was starting to thaw.

00:02:06.373 --> 00:02:08.249
And once it thaws,

00:02:08.249 --> 00:02:11.252
that collapsing ground
can drastically alter

00:02:11.252 --> 00:02:15.590
the vast expanse of the Arctic’s
tundra and boreal forest,

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.177
and it can also threaten
the homes and lifeways

00:02:19.177 --> 00:02:20.553
of Arctic residents.

00:02:21.805 --> 00:02:24.391
Just imagine if the ground
beneath your home

00:02:24.391 --> 00:02:25.975
suddenly started to sink.

00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:29.479
That's what's happening
across the Arctic.

00:02:31.898 --> 00:02:35.819
But thawing permafrost
also threatens everyone on the planet

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:40.156
because it stores a massive amount
of ancient frozen carbon.

00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:41.783
And when that carbon thaws,

00:02:41.783 --> 00:02:44.953
it can be released into the atmosphere
as greenhouse gases,

00:02:44.953 --> 00:02:47.664
leading to more warming and more thaw.

00:02:49.749 --> 00:02:53.044
So let me place the magnitude
of this problem in perspective for you.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.296
By the end of this century,

00:02:55.296 --> 00:02:58.133
greenhouse gas emissions
from thawing permafrost

00:02:58.133 --> 00:03:03.096
may be on par with some of the world’s
leading greenhouse-gas-emitting nations,

00:03:03.096 --> 00:03:08.059
perhaps as large as or larger
than emissions from the United States,

00:03:08.059 --> 00:03:12.313
the second-largest greenhouse-gas-emitting
country in the world.

00:03:14.232 --> 00:03:17.235
And you know, I want to point out,
this is not a new phenomena.

00:03:17.485 --> 00:03:19.487
Arctic residents and scientists

00:03:19.487 --> 00:03:22.490
have been observing
permafrost thaw now for decades.

00:03:23.658 --> 00:03:26.286
But the scale of the research
hasn’t been sufficient

00:03:26.286 --> 00:03:28.538
to meet this enormous challenge.

00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:34.461
Because no one country is directly
responsible for permafrost thaw,

00:03:34.461 --> 00:03:38.381
no single nation has taken
responsibility for fully monitoring

00:03:38.381 --> 00:03:41.509
and tracking its impact across the Arctic.

00:03:42.552 --> 00:03:45.722
And this is not a case
where ignorance is bliss,

00:03:45.722 --> 00:03:49.225
because what doesn't get measured
doesn't get accounted for.

00:03:50.727 --> 00:03:54.939
Because we can’t put a precise number
on permafrost emissions,

00:03:54.939 --> 00:03:58.026
policymakers are essentially
excluding them,

00:03:58.026 --> 00:04:01.446
setting global emissions targets
that are wholly insufficient

00:04:01.446 --> 00:04:04.032
to protect us from catastrophic
climate change.

00:04:05.283 --> 00:04:06.618
Ignoring permafrost

00:04:06.618 --> 00:04:10.413
is essentially like leaving a major
greenhouse-gas-emitting country,

00:04:10.413 --> 00:04:11.581
like the United States,

00:04:11.581 --> 00:04:13.458
out of global climate negotiations,

00:04:13.458 --> 00:04:15.126
which is not a good idea.

00:04:16.586 --> 00:04:21.257
What we need to know is where
permafrost is thawing across the Arctic

00:04:21.257 --> 00:04:23.218
and how fast,

00:04:23.218 --> 00:04:25.678
what that means in terms
of greenhouse gas emissions

00:04:25.678 --> 00:04:30.850
and how that will impact our climate
in 10, 50 or 100 years from now.

00:04:31.976 --> 00:04:36.064
Answering these questions requires
a massive and integrated effort

00:04:36.064 --> 00:04:39.609
at a scale that matches
the enormity of this problem.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:43.196
Thankfully, we have a plan.

00:04:43.947 --> 00:04:47.867
Through an ambitious new initiative
called Permafrost Pathways,

00:04:47.867 --> 00:04:52.455
we’ve formed a coalition
of Arctic residents and scientists,

00:04:52.455 --> 00:04:54.123
Indigenous knowledge-holders

00:04:54.123 --> 00:04:56.543
and Arctic and climate policy influencers

00:04:56.543 --> 00:04:59.712
to tackle this problem
with the urgency it deserves.

00:05:00.922 --> 00:05:05.510
Recognizing that we cannot continue
on our current climate trajectory,

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.888
we’ve brought this team
together to think big

00:05:08.888 --> 00:05:10.640
and to act boldly

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.976
and to work across boundaries

00:05:12.976 --> 00:05:15.770
to take us on a new pathway forward.

00:05:16.938 --> 00:05:18.398
So to get started,

00:05:18.398 --> 00:05:20.358
the first thing that we need to do

00:05:20.358 --> 00:05:22.652
is to actually measure 
greenhouse gas emissions

00:05:22.652 --> 00:05:24.195
across the entire Arctic.

00:05:25.613 --> 00:05:29.993
Currently there are only a handful
of monitoring towers across the Arctic

00:05:29.993 --> 00:05:31.077
that run year round

00:05:31.077 --> 00:05:33.621
and that measure
both important greenhouse gases,

00:05:33.621 --> 00:05:35.957
carbon dioxide and methane.

00:05:36.875 --> 00:05:40.461
So we’re working with a team
of international scientific experts

00:05:40.461 --> 00:05:44.340
to strategically identify
and fill these monitoring gaps

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.135
by installing new equipment
across the Arctic

00:05:47.135 --> 00:05:50.179
in remote locations where monitoring
currently doesn’t exist.

00:05:51.681 --> 00:05:54.517
We believe that by installing
just 10 new monitoring sites,

00:05:54.517 --> 00:05:58.479
we can drastically improve
our estimates of permafrost emissions,

00:05:58.479 --> 00:06:00.398
which will remove a major barrier

00:06:00.398 --> 00:06:03.109
to their incorporation
into the global climate policy.

00:06:04.569 --> 00:06:06.988
And we’re coupling
this on-the-ground monitoring

00:06:06.988 --> 00:06:09.198
with high-resolution
satellite observations

00:06:09.198 --> 00:06:11.451
and advanced computer modeling

00:06:11.451 --> 00:06:14.996
so that we can track
the changing Arctic in near real-time

00:06:14.996 --> 00:06:18.374
and accurately project
permafrost emissions into the future.

00:06:19.959 --> 00:06:22.712
But state-of-the-art science
just isn’t enough.

00:06:23.713 --> 00:06:26.841
We need to make sure
that permafrost emissions are counted

00:06:26.841 --> 00:06:28.801
when setting global emissions targets,

00:06:28.801 --> 00:06:32.972
because each year that we fail
to account for permafrost is another year

00:06:32.972 --> 00:06:36.517
that we become unwittingly locked-in
to more severe climate warming.

00:06:37.852 --> 00:06:40.605
So we’re working
with leading policy experts

00:06:40.605 --> 00:06:42.398
to bring this new knowledge

00:06:42.398 --> 00:06:45.652
to the table with the decision-makers
at the highest level

00:06:45.652 --> 00:06:49.447
to make sure that global climate policy
is accounting for permafrost

00:06:49.447 --> 00:06:51.950
and aiming for the right targets.

00:06:54.494 --> 00:06:55.620
But the truth is,

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:59.165
even with the most
ambitious climate action,

00:06:59.165 --> 00:07:01.834
some permafrost is going to thaw --

00:07:01.834 --> 00:07:03.086
that’s already happening.

00:07:04.212 --> 00:07:06.172
Right now across the Arctic,

00:07:06.172 --> 00:07:10.385
people are having to make
extremely difficult decisions

00:07:10.385 --> 00:07:12.679
about where and how to live

00:07:12.679 --> 00:07:15.306
in order to protect
themselves and their families

00:07:15.306 --> 00:07:17.225
from the hazards of climate change.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.144
So we’re working with our team members

00:07:20.144 --> 00:07:22.689
and our partners who live on permafrost

00:07:22.689 --> 00:07:25.483
to bring these monitoring
and modeling tools

00:07:25.483 --> 00:07:29.946
to assess the current and future impacts
of permafrost thaw on Arctic communities,

00:07:29.946 --> 00:07:32.323
and to co-create adaptation plans

00:07:32.323 --> 00:07:35.576
that protect and respect the health,

00:07:35.576 --> 00:07:36.619
well-being

00:07:36.619 --> 00:07:38.913
and human rights of Arctic residents.

00:07:40.915 --> 00:07:43.001
These are the gaps we plan to fill.

00:07:44.002 --> 00:07:45.920
Painting a picture of the Arctic

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:49.465
that we have never
been able to see before,

00:07:49.465 --> 00:07:52.010
and recognizing that ultimately,

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:55.179
the climate crisis
is a human rights crisis,

00:07:55.179 --> 00:07:57.515
and it’s one that’s already underway.

00:07:58.516 --> 00:08:01.019
But through these actions
that we take now,

00:08:01.019 --> 00:08:03.980
we can greatly reduce future harm

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:07.817
and take us on a more just
and equitable journey.

00:08:08.484 --> 00:08:09.569
Thank you.

00:08:09.569 --> 00:08:10.570
(Applause and cheers)

